{"author":{"id":"f943aa1c5ee078b881a8e97535a4e2ccb401d01ca00f4128126befa9cca58a32"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792555632,"nonce":"HF0phyd2h3qys0mLiJwOYzk/QqE=","metadata":{"github-url":"https://github.com/guilbep/git-board/issues/29","origin":"github","sync:github.com/guilbep/git-board:id":"I_kwDOUJip488AAAABYuT_-Q","sync:github.com/guilbep/git-board:labels":"","sync:github.com/guilbep/git-board:number":"29","sync:github.com/guilbep/git-board:status":"open","sync:github.com/guilbep/git-board:text":"3ee8365e0f14ae0797650b27e0f3b6678ba3ff6369c43be24b7f806c33c13a0c","sync:github.com/guilbep/git-board:title":"github: add EPFL-ENAC's issue types and settle its Priority field","sync:github.com/guilbep/git-board:url":"https://github.com/guilbep/git-board/issues/29"},"title":"github: add EPFL-ENAC's issue types and settle its Priority field","message":"The fork's `native-fields` build turns kinds into GitHub issue types where EPFL-ENAC has one, and keeps them as labels otherwise. This is what the build needs from you, as an org owner or with one. The decision doc is `doc/design/bridges/native-fields.md` on the fork's local `native-fields` branch, which isn't pushed.\n\n## 1. Issue types in EPFL-ENAC\n\nSettings › Planning › Issue types › Create new type. All six are optional: until a type exists, its kind stays a label on GitHub, and nothing breaks. Each type shows in the type picker of every EPFL-ENAC repository (31 use types today), so it's the organization's call. With all six, the org has 9 of the 25 types allowed.\n\n- [ ] **Polish**: A small improvement to something that works\n- [ ] **Performance**: Faster or lighter, same behaviour\n- [ ] **Refactor**: A code change with no change in behaviour\n- [ ] **Documentation**: Docs only\n- [ ] **Style**: Formatting only\n- [ ] **Test**: Tests only\n\nAlready there: **Bug**, **Feature** and **Task**. Task has no git-board kind, so it comes in as `kind:task`.\n\nco2's kinds are flat labels, so once the types exist its bridges need:\n\n```\nkind-types = bug=Bug,feat=Feature,polish=Polish,perf=Performance,refactor=Refactor,docs=Documentation,style=Style,test=Test\n```\n\ngit-board will write that key; nothing to do by hand.\n\n## 2. The Priority issue field\n\nEPFL-ENAC already has organization issue fields: **Priority** (Urgent, High, Medium, Low), **Effort** (High, Medium, Low), **Start date** and **Target date**.\n\n- [ ] Check that Priority's four options fit `prio:p0`–`p3` (p0 = Urgent, p1 = High, p2 = Medium, p3 = Low). Complete it if you want other names or descriptions.\n- [ ] Decide whether `prio:` should become that field natively, like kinds. It would be a later build, not part of `native-fields`.\n\n## 3. A scratch repository for the live test\n\n- [ ] A **private** repository in EPFL-ENAC, for example `EPFL-ENAC/git-bug-native-fields-test`\n- [ ] guilbep has write access to it\n- [ ] The token the bridge uses can reach it. A fine-grained token needs the repository, Issues read and write, Metadata read, and an admin's approval if the org asks for one; a classic token needs `repo`.\n- [ ] It stays out of Project 40 and its automation\n\n## Facts behind it (checked 2026-10-21)\n\n- A user-owned repository has no issue types: `guilbep/git-bug-sync-test` answers `issueTypes: null`. So the live test needs an organization repository.\n- EPFL-ENAC has 542 typed issues in 31 repositories; 65 are in co2-calculator (28 Bug, 33 Feature, 4 Task).\n- gitlab.epfl.ch (19.4.2, free edition) has milestones but no iterations. Sprints become existing milestones there; nothing to add.\n\nDone when the types you want exist, the Priority decision is written here, and the scratch repository exists. Then the `native-fields` session runs the live test.","files":null}]}